/* lessons.js — Study Room notes, condensed from the CLN4U slide decks (one entry per lesson).
   Each lesson: {u, l, title, sub, sections:[{h, items:[...]}]}. Reading time is computed from word count. */
window.LESSONS = [
/* ═════════ UNIT 1 — THE LEGAL MIND ═════════ */
{u:1,l:1,title:"What Is Law?",sub:"Jurisprudence, philosophy, morals, and the social contract",sections:[
 {h:"Key terms",items:["Jurisprudence — the study, knowledge, and science of law. It asks not just 'what is the law?' but 'why do we have law at all?' It sits at the intersection of philosophy, history, and political science.","Philosophy — the field of research that looks into the bigger, more gray areas of our existence: What is real? What is good? How should we live?","Morals — beliefs about what is right or wrong that guide us; shaped by culture, religion, family, and experience. Morals differ between individuals, so law must decide whose morals count, and how.","Moral philosophy — the subsection of philosophy that specifically looks into morals and tries to work out systematic answers to 'what makes an action right or wrong?'","Formal laws — laws that are written down and enforced in a specific way (e.g. the Criminal Code, the Highway Traffic Act), in contrast to informal norms like etiquette.","Social contract — the agreement people make with governments to give up some freedoms in exchange for security. The core justification for why a government has the right to make and enforce law at all."]},
 {h:"A very short history of 'why law?'",items:["Ancient Greece (c. 350 BCE): Aristotle argued law should aim at the common good and distinguished 'natural justice' from rules that vary by convention.","Medieval Europe (13th c.): Thomas Aquinas fused Aristotle with Christian theology — human law must reflect a higher, discoverable moral order (early Natural Law).","17th–18th c.: Hobbes, Locke, and Rousseau asked why people would agree to be governed at all — social contract theory.","20th c.: H.L.A. Hart and Lon Fuller revived the Natural Law vs. Positivism debate after the Nuremberg Trials."]},
 {h:"The three names behind 'social contract'",items:["Thomas Hobbes, Leviathan (1651): without government, life would be a 'war of all against all' — people trade total freedom for safety under a powerful ruler.","John Locke, Two Treatises of Government (1689): people are born with natural rights — life, liberty, and property — and government exists only to protect them. Locke's version directly influenced the American Declaration of Independence and, indirectly, the rights language in Canada's Charter.","Jean-Jacques Rousseau, The Social Contract (1762): legitimate authority comes from the 'general will' of the people, not from a ruler's power alone.","All three agreed that government's authority comes from an agreement with the people, not from force alone."]},
 {h:"Keep asking",items:["Can something be immoral but still legal? Moral but still illegal?","Whose morals should formal laws be based on when people disagree? Should the majority always decide? Are some morals so widely shared they belong in law regardless (e.g. murder)?"]}
]},
{u:1,l:2,title:"Moral & Legal Reasoning",sub:"Two ways of judging an action, and four schools of thought about where law's authority comes from",sections:[
 {h:"Consequential vs. categorical reasoning",items:["Consequential — an action is good or bad depending on its outcome; the ends can justify (or condemn) the means. Example: lying is acceptable if it prevents serious harm.","Categorical — actions have a specific moral value regardless of outcome; some acts are simply right or wrong in themselves. Example: lying is always wrong, even if it prevents harm.","The Trolley Problem (Philippa Foot, 1967): most people would pull the lever (5 saved > 1 lost — consequential) but would not push a stranger off a bridge (using a person as a means feels categorically wrong). Courts face real versions in self-defence, necessity, and triage."]},
 {h:"Four schools of legal thought",items:["Natural Law — the world follows natural rules that are always morally correct or incorrect; law's authority comes from higher reasoning, and unjust laws deserve no credence.","Legal Positivism — laws should be followed because they exist; their authority comes from the government. Laws don't need to be moral to be valid.","Critical Legal Theory — law is created by the elite and carries an inherent bias toward them; the elite is unlikely to extend power to other groups unless it serves their interest.","Legal Realism — a subsection of positivism: morals vary from person to person, so law is shaped by the interpretation of judges and experienced professionals in real cases.","Must law be moral? Natural Law: yes. Positivism, CLT, Realism: no."]},
 {h:"The Nuremberg Trials (1945–46)",items:["Allied powers tried senior Nazi officials for war crimes and crimes against humanity. Many argued they had only followed their own government's laws — a Legal Positivist defence.","The tribunal rejected this: some acts are crimes regardless of what domestic law permitted. Gustav Radbruch later formalized it — 'extreme injustice is no law' (a restatement of Natural Law).","Nuremberg stages the Natural Law vs. Positivism debate in its starkest form: were the Nazi laws 'technically valid' (positivism) or 'never truly law at all' (natural law)?"]},
 {h:"R. v. Latimer (SCC 2001)",items:["Robert Latimer, a Saskatchewan farmer, ended the life of his 12-year-old daughter Tracy (severe cerebral palsy, ongoing pain) in 1993 and argued a 'defence of necessity' — mercy.","A jury convicted him of second-degree murder; in 2001 the Supreme Court unanimously rejected the necessity defence and upheld the conviction and mandatory minimum sentence.","Some judged the act by its compassionate intent and result (consequential); the Court held that intentionally ending a life is categorically wrong regardless of motive — categorical reasoning winning out in Canadian law."]}
]},
{u:1,l:3,title:"Foundations of Canadian Law (Part 1)",sub:"Rule of Law, the principles of justice, and the Magna Carta, 1215",sections:[
 {h:"Rule of Law",items:["No one can operate in a capacity above the set laws of a jurisdiction — ordinary citizens, police officers, and elected officials alike. Without it, those in power could act without legal limits. It is the foundation every other principle of justice builds on."]},
 {h:"Roncarelli v. Duplessis (SCC 1959)",items:["Frank Roncarelli, a Montreal restaurant owner and Jehovah's Witness, posted bail for fellow Witnesses arrested for distributing pamphlets criticizing the Catholic Church.","Maurice Duplessis — Premier and Attorney General of Quebec — personally ordered the Liquor Commission to revoke Roncarelli's licence as punishment, not for any licensing violation.","The Supreme Court ruled Duplessis acted outside his legal authority and ordered him to personally pay over $33,000 in damages.","Canada's clearest rule-of-law case: even the most powerful office holder in Quebec could not use government power for personal or political revenge. 'All discretionary power has legal limits' — decided decades before the Charter existed."]},
 {h:"Four principles of justice",items:["Impartiality — decisions in court should be based on objective criteria rather than personal opinion, bias, or prejudice.","Equality — government and individuals are subject to the same laws and entitled to equal protection of the law.","Innocence until proven guilty — anyone accused of a crime must be presumed innocent until proven guilty in a court of law.","Fairness — the process and procedures of the system: a person should know the evidence the Crown intends to present so they can prepare a defence."]},
 {h:"The Magna Carta, 1215",items:["The 'Great Charter' forced on King John by his barons at Runnymede (June 15, 1215), drafted largely by Archbishop Stephen Langton. It established the radical idea that even a king is bound by the law.","John had lost a war in France and demanded heavy taxes. Ten weeks after sealing, Pope Innocent III annulled the charter at John's request — civil war. After John's death in 1216, his son's regents reissued a revised version, which is the one that endured.","Rights granted: trial by jury of peers; habeas corpus (freedom from unlawful arrest or detainment without evidence); women's rights (a widow could not be forced to remarry or give up property); Rule of Law (nobody is above the law, including the monarch).","Lord Denning (1956): 'the greatest constitutional document of all time — the foundation of the freedom of the individual against the arbitrary authority of the despot.'"]}
]},
{u:1,l:4,title:"Foundations of Canadian Law (Part 2)",sub:"The Bill of Rights, the Charter, the Oakes test, and common law vs. civil law",sections:[
 {h:"The Canadian Bill of Rights, 1960",items:["A federal statute championed by Prime Minister John Diefenbaker — Canada's first rights document, protecting life, liberty, security of the person, and equality before the law.","Crucial limitation: it was an ordinary statute, not part of the Constitution. Parliament could override or amend it with a simple majority, and it did not apply to the provinces."]},
 {h:"The Persons Case (Edwards v. Canada, 1929)",items:["Under the British North America Act, women were not 'qualified persons' and could not be appointed to the Senate. The Famous Five — Emily Murphy, Nellie McClung, Irene Parlby, Louise McKinney, Henrietta Muir Edwards — challenged this.","In 1928 the Supreme Court of Canada ruled women were NOT persons. The Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in Britain (then Canada's highest court of appeal) overturned this on October 18, 1929.","Shows how slow rights progress was without an entrenched rights document."]},
 {h:"The Charter of Rights and Freedoms, 1982",items:["Entrenched in the Constitution Act, 1982 — constitutional law that cannot be changed by an ordinary act of Parliament, binding federal, provincial, and territorial governments.","Protects fundamental freedoms (s.2), democratic rights (s.3–5), legal rights (s.7–14), and equality rights (s.15).","Section 33, the 'notwithstanding clause', lets a legislature override certain Charter rights for up to five years — included in 1982 as a compromise to get the provinces to agree."]},
 {h:"R. v. Oakes (SCC 1986) and the Oakes test",items:["David Oakes was charged under the Narcotic Control Act, which presumed anyone in possession of drugs was trafficking unless they proved otherwise — a 'reverse onus' violating s.11(d), the presumption of innocence.","The Court struck down the law and created the Oakes test for whether a violation is justified under section 1 ('reasonable limits'): (1) is the government's goal pressing and substantial? (2) are the means proportional — rationally connected, minimally impairing, and worth the cost to the right?","Used in nearly every Charter case since 1986."]},
 {h:"Common law vs. civil law",items:["Common law — not written in legislation; it evolves from judicial decisions, built on precedent (stare decisis). Adaptable; used across Canada outside Québec's private law.","Civil law — can mean (1) law that is written down, like the Charter, or (2) private-law matters such as contracts. Québec's private law is based on a Civil Code dating back to the Napoleonic Code. More predictable, since rules are set out in a comprehensive code."]}
]},
{u:1,l:5,title:"How Law Is Made in Canada",sub:"From legislature to Royal Assent",sections:[
 {h:"Structures of government",items:["Legislature — the building and the body of elected officials where laws are created.","Unicameral — one house (e.g. Queen's Park, Ontario). Bicameral — two houses (Parliament in Ottawa: House of Commons + Senate).","Parliament — the House of Commons, the Senate, and the Monarch (represented by the Governor General).","Federation — a union of provinces and territories governed together under one central (federal) government.","Statute / legislation / act — written law passed by a legislature, distinct from unwritten, precedent-based common law."]},
 {h:"Where Parliament comes from",items:["Modelled on the British Westminster system. The Constitution Act, 1867 (originally the British North America Act) created the Dominion of Canada, the House of Commons, and the Senate.","Canada's highest court of appeal was the Judicial Committee of the Privy Council in Britain until 1949; Canada has been fully independent since 1982. Royal Assent survives as a legacy of the British origin."]},
 {h:"Royal Assent and regulations",items:["Royal Assent — the Monarch or their representative signs a bill into law: federally the Governor General, provincially the Lieutenant Governor. Both are appointed, not elected.","Regulations — carry out or expand on the details of general laws; created by government departments under authority already granted by a statute, not by a full legislative vote. The statute sets the goal; regulations fill in operational detail."]},
 {h:"How a federal bill becomes law",items:["1. First Reading — introduced in the House of Commons; no debate yet.","2. Second Reading & Committee — MPs debate the principle; a committee studies it in detail and may amend it.","3. Third Reading — the House debates and votes on the final version.","4. The Senate — the bill repeats a similar process in Parliament's second chamber.","5. Royal Assent — the Governor General signs the bill into law."]},
 {h:"The Cannabis Act — Bill C-45",items:["First Reading April 13, 2017 → Third Reading passed November 27, 2017 → Senate debate and amendments March–June 2018 → final Senate passage June 19, 2018 → Royal Assent June 21, 2018 → in force October 17, 2018 (nearly four months later, giving provinces time to prepare).","About 18 months from First Reading to coming into force — normal for major, debated legislation."]}
]},
{u:1,l:6,title:"Justice System Roles & Ethics",sub:"Vocabulary for legal news, the SIU, and equity vs. equality",sections:[
 {h:"Vocabulary for legal news",items:["SIU — Ontario's Special Investigations Unit, a civilian agency that investigates incidents involving police and the public.","Pro bono — 'for free'; legal services donated without charge.","Accountability — the obligation or willingness to accept responsibility for one's actions.","Intervene — to come between, prevent, or alter a result. Imperative — very important; must be done.","Allegation — a claim that someone has done something wrong or illegal — not yet proven.","Integrity — honesty and strong moral principles. Anathema — something or someone very strongly disliked.","Impartial — treating all people and groups equally, without favouritism or bias. Rebuke — a sharp expression of criticism."]},
 {h:"The Special Investigations Unit (1990)",items:["Ontario was the first province in Canada — and one of the first jurisdictions anywhere — to create an independent civilian body to investigate police conduct, after years of advocacy by community organizations including the Black Action Defence Committee following police-involved deaths in the 1980s.","Investigates any police incident resulting in death, serious injury, or an allegation of sexual assault, and can lay criminal charges. Motto: 'One Law for All' — an echo of the rule of law.","Answers the tension: police enforce the law, but who investigates them if they break it?"]},
 {h:"Equity vs. equality",items:["Equality — sameness: everyone gets the exact same thing (every student writes the same open-book test). Simple, but ignores different starting points.","Equity — fairness: giving people access to the same opportunities even if that means different treatment (a student with an IEP receives an accommodation). Aims for equal outcome or opportunity, not identical treatment."]},
 {h:"The AODA (2005)",items:["The Accessibility for Ontarians with Disabilities Act set out to make Ontario fully accessible by 2025 through enforceable standards in customer service, employment, transportation, and the built environment.","It does not require every building to be identical — it requires whatever accommodation is needed so everyone can access the same outcome. Ontario law built on equity, not equality."]}
]},
{u:1,l:7,title:"Investigating Contemporary Issues",sub:"The CRAAP test and current legal issues",sections:[
 {h:"The CRAAP test",items:["A five-part checklist for deciding whether a source is trustworthy. A source can fail on just one criterion and still be misleading — check all five. It is the same standard journalists, lawyers, and researchers use.","Currency — how recently was it published or updated? Law changes; an outdated source may describe a repealed law.","Relevance — does it address your topic at the right depth?","Authority — who is the author or publisher? Do they have real expertise?","Accuracy — is it supported by evidence and verifiable against other reliable sources?","Purpose — why does it exist: to inform, persuade, sell, or entertain? Bias affects reliability."]},
 {h:"Where CRAAP came from",items:["Developed in 2010 by Sarah Blakeslee and librarians at the Meriam Library, California State University, Chico, to help students tell reliable from unreliable sources online. Released under an open licence; memorable, teachable in a class, works on almost any source.","Even a tool for judging authority has its own history and authors — you could evaluate CRAAP using CRAAP."]},
 {h:"Five legal issues in the news",items:["The notwithstanding clause — Alberta invoked s.33 to shield laws affecting transgender youth from court challenge.","The Online Harms Act — Bill C-63 died on the order paper in 2025, though the government says its commitment remains.","ICJ climate ruling — in 2025 the International Court of Justice ruled states have binding duties under international law to limit emissions.","Sanctions on ICC officials — the U.S. sanctioned ICC judges and prosecutors in 2025 over war crimes investigations.","Mi'kmaq fishery dispute — a legal challenge over a Mi'kmaq-led fishery reignited debate over Indigenous treaty rights in Atlantic Canada."]}
]},

/* ═════════ UNIT 2 — LEGAL FOUNDATIONS ═════════ */
{u:2,l:1,title:"The Skin I'm In & Racial Profiling",sub:"Desmond Cole, carding, and R. v. Brown",sections:[
 {h:"Desmond Cole and 'The Skin I'm In' (Toronto Life, May 2015)",items:["Toronto journalist, author, and activist against anti-Black racism in policing. His cover story documented over 50 times he had been stopped, questioned, and 'carded' by Toronto police despite no suspicion of any crime.","Carding — police stopping someone, asking for ID, and recording their information, even with no suspicion of a crime.","Cole was roughly 17 times more likely to be carded in downtown Toronto than a white resident. Black people made up about 9.3% of the Canadian prison population despite being roughly 2.9% of the general population.","'Areas where black people live are heavily policed in the name of crime prevention, which opens up everyone in that neighbourhood to disproportionate scrutiny.' Later expanded into the 2017 documentary and 2020 book The Skin We're In."]},
 {h:"R. v. Brown (Ontario Court of Appeal, 2003)",items:["Toronto police stopped Decovan 'Dee' Brown, a Toronto Raptors player, on the Don Valley Parkway (Nov. 1, 1999). He failed a roadside breath test but argued he was stopped without real cause as a Black man in an expensive car — arbitrary detention under s.9.","The trial judge dismissed the profiling argument; the appeal court found the judge's conduct showed bias against even considering it, and in 2003 ordered a new trial.","First Canadian appellate decision to formally recognize racial profiling: 'criminal profiling based on race… race is illegitimately used as a proxy for the criminality or general criminal propensity of an entire racial group.' Profiling can be proven through circumstantial evidence and inference, since an officer is unlikely to admit it."]},
 {h:"Vocabulary",items:["Discretionary — left to individual judgment rather than fixed rule. Arbitrary — based on whim rather than reason, evidence, or law. Articulable cause — a clear, specific, describable reason beyond a hunch that can justify a stop or search.","Infringe — to violate a right. Testify — give evidence as a witness under oath. Counsel — a lawyer representing a person. Cross-examination — questioning of a witness by the opposing side. Disrepute — low public esteem; used in the Charter for conduct that damages confidence in the justice system.","Summary offence — less serious, tried quickly without a jury, lighter penalties; summary conviction — decided by a judge alone. Indictable offence — more serious, heavier penalties, may involve a jury. Allegation — a claim not yet proven.","Reasonable limits — the Charter's section 1 standard: rights can be limited only by a law demonstrably justified in a free and democratic society."]}
]},
{u:2,l:2,title:"Ontario's Court System",sub:"Court of Appeal · Superior Court of Justice · Ontario Court of Justice · tribunals",sections:[
 {h:"The Court of Appeal for Ontario",items:["The highest court in the province, seated at Osgoode Hall in Toronto. A federally appointed Chief Justice of Ontario, an Associate Chief Justice, and roughly 20+ judges; appeals heard by one or three judges. Further appeal goes only to the Supreme Court of Canada.","An appeal is not a new trial — appeal courts review whether the law was applied correctly; they don't re-hear witnesses or re-decide the facts."]},
 {h:"The Superior Court of Justice",items:["Ontario's trial court of general jurisdiction: the most serious criminal matters, major civil lawsuits, and complex family law. Over 300 federally appointed judges in 52 locations.","Three branches: Divisional Court (appeals and judicial reviews of government agencies, boards, and tribunals, plus some civil appeals up to $50,000); Family Court (divorce, property, support, custody in regions with a Unified Family Court); Small Claims Court (civil claims of $50,000 or less, mostly heard by deputy judges).","Together with the Ontario Court of Justice, sometimes called the 'Court of Ontario'."]},
 {h:"The Ontario Court of Justice",items:["The lower provincial court — judges and justices of the peace appointed provincially. Hears provincial offences (e.g. Highway Traffic Act), most criminal matters, and many family matters; acts as youth court under the Youth Criminal Justice Act. Some municipal locations are known as Provincial Offences Courts.","Where there is no Unified Family Court: divorce and property go to the Superior Court; custody, support, and child protection can go to the Ontario Court of Justice."]},
 {h:"Administrative boards and tribunals",items:["Many disputes never go to court — e.g. the Landlord and Tenant Board, the Human Rights Tribunal of Ontario, the Workplace Safety and Insurance Appeals Tribunal. Faster, less formal, specialized; decisions can sometimes be reviewed by the Divisional Court.","Why so many levels? Trial courts establish facts and apply law; appeal courts check the law was applied correctly; specialized branches keep simpler disputes from tying up the resources of a murder trial."]}
]},
{u:2,l:3,title:"Why Do People Obey — or Break — the Law?",sub:"Deterrence, social bonds, socialization, legitimacy, and theories of law-breaking",sections:[
 {h:"Why people obey",items:["Social contract (Unit 1) — one explanation, but not the only one.","Deterrence Theory — people obey because they fear punishment. General deterrence: punishing one offender discourages society at large. Specific deterrence: punishing an offender discourages that person from reoffending.","Social Bonding Theory (Travis Hirschi, 1969) — people conform because of bonds to society: attachment to others, commitment to goals, involvement in activities, and belief in social values. Weaker bonds make law-breaking more likely.","Legal Socialization — family, school, and culture teach us from a young age to follow rules; we internalize legal norms long before we can explain them.","Legitimacy & Procedural Justice (Tom Tyler) — people obey more when they believe the system is fair and legitimate, not just because they fear being caught."]},
 {h:"Why people break the law",items:["Rational Choice Theory — people weigh costs (getting caught, punishment) against benefits and decide the crime is 'worth it'.","Strain Theory (Robert Merton, 1938) — when legitimate paths to success are blocked, some turn to illegitimate means.","Differential Association (Edwin Sutherland, 1939) — criminal behaviour is learned through close relationships with others who model or normalize it.","Necessity — breaking the law out of genuine desperation (poverty, hunger, survival).","No single theory explains all obedience or all law-breaking; real behaviour is shaped by several factors at once. Hirschi's weak bonds and Sutherland's differential association both point to relationships and environment."]}
]},
{u:2,l:4,title:"The Roots and Sources of Canadian Law",sub:"Four roots and thirteen areas of law",sections:[
 {h:"The four roots of Canadian law",items:["British Legal Tradition — Canada inherited its core structure from England: the Magna Carta's rule of law, common law, and the Westminster parliamentary system.","Statute Law — written laws passed by a legislature (federal, provincial, or municipal). Statutes can create new law or override existing common law.","Constitutional Law — the supreme law of Canada: the Constitution Act, 1867, the Constitution Act, 1982, and the Charter. Any conflicting law is invalid.","Case Law — law developed through judicial decisions and precedent (stare decisis); still actively growing.","The roots interact: a court can strike down a statute for violating the Constitution (R. v. Oakes); a new statute can override a judge's decision. Constitutional law sits above the other three."]},
 {h:"Thirteen areas of Canadian law",items:["Administrative (government agencies, boards, tribunals) · Constitutional (structure of government and guaranteed rights) · Criminal (offences against society, in the federal Criminal Code) · Environmental (pollution, resources, land use) · Tax (how people and businesses are taxed; the CRA) · Aboriginal (Indigenous peoples, the Crown, treaty and title rights) · Commercial (business transactions, corporations, trade).","Contract (binding agreements and remedies) · Labour (employers, employees, unions, collective bargaining) · Family (marriage, divorce, custody, support) · Property (ownership, use, transfer of real and personal property) · Tort (civil wrongs like negligence or defamation; compensation for harm) · Wills and Estates (distribution of property after death)."]}
]},
{u:2,l:5,title:"Landmark Cases in Canadian Law",sub:"Delgamuukw · Ewanchuk · Dunmore",sections:[
 {h:"Delgamuukw v. British Columbia (SCC 1997)",items:["Fifty-one hereditary chiefs of the Gitxsan and Wet'suwet'en Nations claimed ownership and jurisdiction over 58,000 km² in northwestern BC, arguing their title predated Confederation and had never been extinguished. The BC Supreme Court rejected the claim, refusing to accept oral histories as evidence.","The Supreme Court ordered a new trial: oral histories must be treated as equally valid evidence, and set out a framework for proving Aboriginal title.","Aboriginal title is a right to the land itself — not just a right to hunt or fish on it. Governments must consult, and sometimes compensate, First Nations before affecting Crown land subject to a title claim."]},
 {h:"R. v. Ewanchuk (SCC 1999)",items:["A 17-year-old woman met Steve Ewanchuk during a job interview in his van; he repeatedly made advances, stopping briefly each time she said no. Frightened, she tried to appear calm and did not physically resist. The trial judge acquitted on 'implied consent'.","The Supreme Court unanimously overturned the acquittal: no defence of implied consent exists in Canadian law. 'No means no.' Consent is what the complainant was actually thinking and feeling — not inferred from silence, failure to resist, or the accused's assumptions.","Justice Claire L'Heureux-Dubé publicly rebuked an Alberta Court of Appeal judge for victim-blaming language, shifting how courts discuss consent. The case shaped how consent is taught, including in Ontario's health curriculum."]},
 {h:"Dunmore v. Ontario (Attorney General) (SCC 2001)",items:["Ontario's Labour Relations Act excluded agricultural workers, leaving them no statutory right to unionize. Tom Dunmore and others argued this violated freedom of association under s.2(d). The government said the exclusion protected family farms.","The Court ruled 7–2 that the exclusion substantially interfered with freedom of association and struck it down, suspending the ruling 18 months for new legislation.","Section 2(d) can impose a positive obligation on government — not just a duty to avoid interfering, but sometimes a duty to actively extend protection so a right can be exercised at all. Most Charter cases ask whether government did something it shouldn't; Dunmore asked whether it failed to do something it should."]}
]},
{u:2,l:6,title:"Comparative Justice: Canada & the United States",sub:"Executive power, due process, and constitutional limits",sections:[
 {h:"Two constitutional systems",items:["Canada — parliamentary: the Prime Minister is a sitting MP chosen by the party controlling the House of Commons. The Charter is supreme law; inconsistent laws are 'of no force and effect' under s.52 of the Constitution Act, 1982. Courts can strike down laws, but s.33 lets a legislature override some rights for up to 5 years.","United States — presidential: strict separation of powers between executive (President), legislative (Congress), and judicial (courts). The Constitution is supreme; judicial review was established in Marbury v. Madison (1803). No equivalent to the notwithstanding clause; amendment requires a difficult supermajority process."]},
 {h:"Trump v. Barbara — birthright citizenship (U.S. Supreme Court, June 30, 2026, 6–3)",items:["A January 2025 executive order sought to deny automatic citizenship to children born on U.S. soil to undocumented or temporary-visa parents. Federal courts blocked it under the 14th Amendment's citizenship clause and over a century of precedent; the Supreme Court struck it down 6–3. A narrower August 2026 order is still being litigated.","Can a president rewrite a constitutional guarantee by executive order alone? Canadian parallel: a rule-of-law question like Roncarelli v. Duplessis; in Canada, any executive action inconsistent with the Constitution is automatically of no force and effect under s.52."]},
 {h:"NPR v. Trump — defunding public media (U.S. District Court, D.C., March 31, 2026)",items:["Executive Order 14290 (May 2025) directed agencies to cut funding to NPR and PBS, citing alleged editorial bias. Judge Randolph Moss ruled the key provision unconstitutional under the First Amendment — government funding cannot be used to punish an outlet's editorial content (viewpoint discrimination).","Canadian parallel: s.2(b) freedom of the press. CBC operates at arm's length from government; a politically motivated defunding would face the Oakes test — a very difficult bar."]},
 {h:"Deportations under the Alien Enemies Act (D.C., Boasberg, C.J., Dec. 2025)",items:["In March 2025 the administration invoked the 1798 Act to deport Venezuelan men alleged to be gang members to CECOT in El Salvador without any hearing. Chief Judge James Boasberg ruled this denied due process and ordered the government to facilitate hearings.","Due process applies to 'any person', not just citizens; an allegation of gang membership is not proof. Canadian parallel: Charter s.7 (fundamental justice), s.9 (arbitrary detention), s.10 (reasons and counsel) — and the principles of innocence until proven guilty and fairness."]},
 {h:"Two more disputes",items:["Control over elections — 2025–26 executive orders on mail-in voting and voter registration were blocked: the Constitution gives that authority to states and Congress, not the president (separation of powers).","Trump v. CASA (June 2025) — the Supreme Court limited 'universal injunctions', making some rights harder to enforce quickly even when a policy is later found unconstitutional.","In every case, a court — not Congress or the President — identified and enforced the constitutional limit. Canada's equivalent is judicial review under the Charter, but s.33 gives legislatures a tool the U.S. lacks."]}
]},

/* ═════════ UNIT 3 — HUMAN RIGHTS ═════════ */
{u:3,l:1,title:"What Are Human Rights?",sub:"Definitions, categories, and a history of the idea",sections:[
 {h:"Defining human rights",items:["Entitlements every person holds simply by virtue of being human — not granted by a government, not earned or revoked as a favour. Different from a privilege or legal benefit, which a government can create, limit, or take away.","Universal (apply to everyone, everywhere), inalienable (cannot be given up or taken away), and indivisible (civil, political, economic, social, and cultural rights are all equally important).","Human rights theory adds to the social contract the claim that some rights exist whether or not any government recognizes them."]},
 {h:"Two categories",items:["Civil and political rights — protect the individual from the state: expression, fair trial, freedom from arbitrary detention.","Economic, social, and cultural rights — protect a person's ability to live with dignity: education, health care, an adequate standard of living, cultural life.","For most of the 20th century, Western nations emphasized the first and the Soviet bloc the second."]},
 {h:"A history of the idea",items:["The Cyrus Cylinder (c. 539 BCE, Persia) describes freeing enslaved people and allowing religious freedom after Cyrus the Great conquered Babylon. Greek and Roman philosophy introduced natural law. Confucian, Islamic, Hindu, and Indigenous traditions independently developed ideas of duty, dignity, and justice.","The Magna Carta (1215) limited a monarch's power; Enlightenment thinkers (Hobbes, Locke, Rousseau) developed natural, inherent rights, shaping the American Declaration of Independence (1776) and the French Declaration of the Rights of Man and of the Citizen (1789) — the first documents to describe rights as universal entitlements of citizens.","Early documents rarely applied to everyone: the French Declaration excluded women and enslaved people; 'all men are created equal' coexisted with slavery. Abolition, women's suffrage, labour movements, and decolonization expanded who rights applied to.","The post-war turning point: WWII and the Holocaust led to the United Nations (1945) and the UDHR (1948). How a government treated its own citizens became a matter of legitimate international concern."]}
]},
{u:3,l:2,title:"The Charter, Section by Section (Part 1)",sub:"Sections 1–14 and their corresponding responsibilities",sections:[
 {h:"Section 1 — reasonable limits",items:["'…subject only to such reasonable limits prescribed by law as can be demonstrably justified in a free and democratic society.' No right is absolute; section 1 is the source of the Oakes test. Every Charter case asks: was a right violated, and can section 1 save it?"]},
 {h:"Section 2 — fundamental freedoms",items:["Conscience and religion; thought, belief, opinion, and expression (including the press); peaceful assembly; association. Without them citizens cannot organize, worship, speak, or criticize government.","R. v. Big M Drug Mart Ltd. (1985) struck down a federal Sunday closing law — the first major freedom-of-religion ruling under s.2(a)."]},
 {h:"Sections 3–6 — democratic and mobility rights",items:["s.3 — every citizen may vote and run for office; s.4 — maximum five-year term for the House of Commons and legislatures; s.5 — Parliament and legislatures must sit at least once every twelve months. These cannot be overridden by s.33.","s.6 — mobility: citizens may enter, remain in, and leave Canada; citizens and permanent residents may move to and work in any province. Also beyond the reach of s.33."]},
 {h:"Sections 7–14 — legal rights",items:["The largest and most litigated cluster: s.7 life, liberty, security of the person; s.8 unreasonable search or seizure; s.9 arbitrary detention; s.10 reasons for arrest and right to counsel; s.11 rights on being charged (trial within a reasonable time, presumption of innocence, jury trial 11(f)); s.12 cruel and unusual punishment; s.13 self-incrimination; s.14 interpreter.","Hunter v. Southam (1984) set the standard for a 'reasonable' search (s.8); R. v. Oakes (1986) is an s.11(d) case; R. v. Jordan (2016) set strict time limits under s.11(b)."]},
 {h:"Every right comes with a responsibility",items:["Right to vote → vote as an informed citizen and respect a fair election's outcome. Jury trial → serve on a jury if summoned. Expression → exercise it without infringing others' rights. Legal rights → respect the legal rights of others, including the presumption of innocence."]}
]},
{u:3,l:3,title:"The Charter, Section by Section (Part 2)",sub:"Equality, language, Indigenous rights, and the limits on limits",sections:[
 {h:"Section 15 — equality rights",items:["Every individual is equal before and under the law, with equal protection and benefit without discrimination based on race, national or ethnic origin, colour, religion, sex, age, or mental or physical disability. The list is not exhaustive — courts have 'read in' grounds like sexual orientation.","Andrews v. Law Society of British Columbia (1989) — the Court's first major s.15 interpretation. Section 15 came into force in 1985, three years after the rest of the Charter, to give governments time to review existing laws."]},
 {h:"Sections 16–23 — language rights",items:["English and French have equal status in Parliament, federal courts, and federal services (s.16–20); minority-language education rights (s.23). Central to Confederation and to protecting French-speaking minorities outside Quebec and the English-speaking minority within it. Section 23 has generated litigation over what level of French schooling provinces must provide."]},
 {h:"Sections 25 & 35 — Aboriginal and treaty rights",items:["s.25 — nothing in the Charter takes away from existing Aboriginal, treaty, or other rights. s.35 (in a separate part of the Constitution Act, 1982) recognizes and affirms existing Aboriginal and treaty rights. These underpin Delgamuukw and R. v. Sparrow (the framework for s.35 rights, via a fishing right)."]},
 {h:"Sections 1, 33, 32, 34 and the amending formula",items:["Section 1 is used constantly; s.33 rarely but deliberately — it can override s.2 and s.7–15 (fundamental freedoms, legal rights, equality) for up to five years, renewable. It cannot override democratic (3–5), mobility (6), or language (16–23) rights.","s.32 — the Charter applies to Parliament, the federal government, and every provincial legislature and government, not private individuals or companies directly. s.34 — the short title.","Amending the Charter requires the Part V formula: typically Parliament plus at least seven provinces representing 50% of the population."]},
 {h:"More rights, more responsibilities",items:["Equality → treat others equally in daily life. Minority language → institutions actively support access. Aboriginal and treaty rights → governments consult meaningfully (Delgamuukw, Sparrow). Section 1 → the state must justify any limit; the burden of proof falls on the state."]}
]},
{u:3,l:4,title:"The Universal Declaration of Human Rights",sub:"Creation, purpose, and the westernization critique",sections:[
 {h:"Adopted by the UN General Assembly, Paris — December 10, 1948",items:["The UN Commission on Human Rights (formed 1946) was chaired by Eleanor Roosevelt. The drafting committee was international: René Cassin (France) wrote the first full draft; Charles Malik (Lebanon) was rapporteur; Peng Chung Chang (China) vice-chair; John Peters Humphrey (Canada) prepared the initial blueprint.","Eighty-five working sessions in under two years produced 30 articles. Of 58 member states: 48 in favour, none against, 8 abstentions (including the Soviet bloc and South Africa), 2 not voting.","The first time the international community agreed that individuals — not just states — hold rights that matter internationally. Later treaties named in the CLN4U curriculum (Convention on the Rights of the Child, Convention on the Rights of Persons with Disabilities, UN Declaration on the Rights of Indigenous Peoples) trace back to it."]},
 {h:"What's in it",items:["Both categories: civil and political rights (life, liberty, freedom from torture, fair trial, expression, religion) and economic, social, and cultural rights (work, education, standard of living, rest and leisure).","Cassin's Greek temple: a foundation (Articles 1–2, dignity and equality), four columns (families of rights), and a pediment (Articles 28–30, the conditions needed to realize these rights and their limits).","A General Assembly resolution — not directly legally binding — but the foundation of nearly every human rights treaty since, including the International Covenant on Civil and Political Rights and the International Covenant on Economic, Social and Cultural Rights, which made its principles binding. Canada has signed both."]},
 {h:"The westernization critique",items:["Critics argue the UDHR reflects Western liberal values — individual, civil, and political rights — rather than universal ones, drafted in the aftermath of European colonialism. Some invoke cultural relativism: rights standards should be judged relative to each culture's own values.","Strongest counter-argument: the drafting committee was genuinely international, and delegates like Lakshmi Menon of India and P.C. Chang of China pushed back against Western-only framing.","Signing or ratifying a treaty is an international commitment, but enforcement varies — from binding tribunals to little more than reputational pressure."]}
]},
{u:3,l:5,title:"Universalism vs. Relativism: Understanding Situatedness",sub:"A framework for cross-cultural legal thinking",sections:[
 {h:"Situatedness",items:["Our moral judgments are shaped by the specific cultural, historical, and social position we occupy — we don't judge from nowhere, we judge from somewhere.","Recognizing situatedness does NOT mean abandoning judgment. It means pausing to ask: is my reaction based on careful reasoning, or on what simply feels familiar? Considering fairness and justice requires understanding whose fairness and whose justice is being assumed."]},
 {h:"A graduated spectrum — three examples, not one",items:["Tier 1 — infant ear piercing: widely practised across Latin American, South Asian, and Middle Eastern cultures; a body modification on a child who cannot consent. International law standing: not classified as a human rights concern by any major body — no lasting harm; a benign tradition.","Tier 2 — male circumcision: practised for religious reasons in Judaism (brit milah) and Islam (khitan), and common as a secular medical practice in the United States — which breaks the assumption that this is a 'non-Western' practice judged by 'the West'. Some bioethicists ('intactivists') argue it violates bodily autonomy. Standing: not classified as a violation by the WHO or UN; genuinely debated without legal consensus on harm.","Tier 3 — female genital cutting: framed within communities as tradition or rite of passage. The anthropological critique: outside condemnation can echo colonial moral policing; lasting change comes from community-led education. Standing: the WHO and UN classify it as a human rights violation — documented physical and psychological harm, no medical benefit, lifelong consequences."]},
 {h:"What's actually doing the work?",items:["International law draws its line on documented physical harm, whether the practice has any recognized medical benefit, and whether the harm is permanent or reversible — not on how unfamiliar a practice feels to an outside observer.","Treating every example as equally debatable would misrepresent what international law actually says."]}
]},
{u:3,l:6,title:"Historical Abuses: Mandela & Rwanda",sub:"Apartheid, the Rwandan genocide, and the failure to intervene",sections:[
 {h:"Nelson Mandela and apartheid (South Africa, 1948–1994)",items:["Apartheid — a legal system of racial segregation enforced from 1948 to the early 1990s, classifying people by race and restricting where Black South Africans could live, work, and move.","Mandela, a leader of the African National Congress, was arrested in 1962, charged with sabotage, and spent 27 years in prison, mostly on Robben Island. International sanctions and a global anti-apartheid movement, plus internal resistance, forced negotiations. Released in 1990; negotiated the end of apartheid with F.W. de Klerk (shared 1993 Nobel Peace Prize); elected first Black president in 1994.","Reconciliation over retribution: the Truth and Reconciliation Commission offered amnesty in exchange for full public disclosure of apartheid-era crimes — controversial, since some victims felt it prioritized national unity over individual justice. Invictus (2009) dramatizes the 1995 Rugby World Cup as a lens on nation-building."]},
 {h:"The Rwandan genocide (April–July 1994)",items:["Colonial-era authorities divided the population into Hutu and Tutsi categories that hardened into lasting political division. On April 6, 1994, a plane carrying Rwanda's president was shot down; within hours extremist Hutu militias began a planned campaign targeting Tutsi civilians and moderate Hutu.","Over about 100 days, an estimated 800,000 people were killed. A small UN peacekeeping force under Canadian General Roméo Dallaire was present but not reinforced or given a mandate to intervene; the international community largely failed to act despite early warnings.","Hotel Rwanda (2004) depicts Paul Rusesabagina sheltering over a thousand refugees at the Hôtel des Mille Collines in Kigali. The failure shaped the UN's 'Responsibility to Protect' doctrine and remains a central case study on when the international community should intervene — sovereignty versus the duty to prevent atrocities."]}
]},
{u:3,l:7,title:"Contemporary Abuses I: Malala & the Hidden Labour Behind AI",sub:"Education rights, workers' rights, and the global digital economy",sections:[
 {h:"Malala Yousafzai (Swat Valley, Pakistan)",items:["Began advocating for girls' education as a teenager after the Taliban banned girls from school in the Swat Valley. On October 9, 2012, at 15, she was shot by a Taliban gunman on a school bus in retaliation. She survived after treatment in Pakistan and the UK and continued internationally.","In 2014, at 17, she became the youngest-ever Nobel Peace Prize laureate, sharing it with Kailash Satyarthi. Her case illustrates UDHR Article 26 (education) and Article 19 (expression) under violent attack, and the risks human rights defenders face. The Malala Fund shows a right declared in principle still needing work to be realized."]},
 {h:"The hidden labour behind ChatGPT (OpenAI & Sama, Nairobi, 2021–22)",items:["AI tools are trained on enormous datasets that include violent, hateful, and sexually explicit material; someone must label it so it can be filtered out. OpenAI contracted Sama, a Kenya-based outsourcing firm, to have workers read and label tens of thousands of such passages.","Workers took home roughly $1.32–$2 per hour while OpenAI paid Sama about $12.50 per hour. Workers described lasting psychological effects with limited, often group-only mental health support. Sama ended the contracts early amid internal concerns and public scrutiny.","Connects to UDHR Article 23 (just and favourable conditions of work, fair remuneration) and Article 5 (cruel, inhuman, or degrading treatment), and to Unit 2's labour law: Global South workers doing the most difficult work behind Global North technology without matching pay or protections.","Malala's case became visible instantly; the AI case took an investigative report to surface. Who bears responsibility — OpenAI, Sama, or both? Does paying above local minimum wage settle the ethical question?"]}
]},
{u:3,l:8,title:"Contemporary Abuses II: ICE & Guantánamo Bay",sub:"Applying the UDHR to current U.S. detention practices",sections:[
 {h:"Conditions in U.S. immigration detention (2025–26)",items:["Human Rights Watch reported 52 deaths in ICE custody in the first 500 days of the current administration — a 140% increase in mortality rate. In 2025 the DHS Office for Civil Rights and Civil Liberties was cut from roughly 150 staff to a small group, sharply reducing independent monitoring.","A 2025 HRW investigation documented allegations at Florida centres: denial of medical care, overcrowding, people shackled for extended periods. A U.S. Senate investigation (Senator Jon Ossoff, January 2026) identified over 1,000 credible reports of abuse since January 2025, including mistreatment of pregnant women and children and denial of food or water.","UDHR Article 3 (life, liberty, security), Article 5 (cruel treatment), Article 25 (adequate standard of living, medical care). In Canada: Charter s.7 and s.12. A recurring theme: reduced oversight alongside reports of harm."]},
 {h:"Guantánamo Bay and The Mauritanian",items:["The U.S. military prison at Guantánamo Bay, Cuba opened in January 2002; of roughly 780 men held, only a small number were ever convicted.","Mohamedou Ould Slahi was detained by Mauritanian authorities at U.S. request in November 2001, transferred through Jordan and Afghanistan, and sent to Guantánamo in August 2002 — never charged. He was subjected to torture approved by Donald Rumsfeld as a 'Special Project': prolonged isolation, sleep deprivation, threats against him and his mother. A federal judge ruled his detention unlawful in 2010; the government appealed and he was held six more years, released October 2016 after 14 years. His memoir Guantánamo Diary became the 2021 film The Mauritanian.","UDHR Article 9 (arbitrary detention), Article 5 (torture), and Article 10 (fair and public hearing) all violated in one case. Since February 2025 the U.S. has also transferred immigration detainees there — connecting back to the Alien Enemies Act deportations from Unit 2.","Across Unit 2's comparative justice lesson and this one: due process denied, oversight reduced, and international human rights standards invoked as the framework for evaluation."]}
]},

/* ═════════ UNIT 4 — CRIME AND CRIMINAL LAW ═════════ */
{u:4,l:1,title:"Foundations of Criminal Law",sub:"Elements of a crime, classifying offences, and culture",sections:[
 {h:"The two elements of a crime",items:["Actus reus ('guilty act') — the physical act of committing an offence; without a prohibited act there is no crime.","Mens rea ('guilty mind') — the mental intent; most serious crimes require proof the accused meant to commit the act, or was at least reckless or criminally negligent.","Both must generally be proven together beyond a reasonable doubt. Strict liability offences — most provincial offences like speeding — require no mens rea at all."]},
 {h:"Classifying offences",items:["By severity: summary (less serious, no jury, lighter penalty); indictable (more serious, may involve a jury, heavier penalty); hybrid (the Crown chooses summary or indictment based on the case).","By type of harm: against the person (assault, homicide, sexual offences); against property (theft, break and enter, fraud); against public order and morality (obstruction of justice, public mischief)."]},
 {h:"Morality, culture, and the definition of crime",items:["What counts as a crime reflects a society's values at a given moment and varies across countries and time. Assisted suicide was criminal in Canada until Carter v. Canada (2015) found the prohibition violated s.7; drinking age, gambling, and cannabis regulation vary by country and province."]},
 {h:"Vocabulary",items:["Culpable homicide — a killing the law holds someone criminally responsible for (murder, manslaughter). Non-culpable homicide — a killing that is not a crime (justified self-defence, accident without negligence).","Tunnel vision — investigators focus on one suspect early and interpret all evidence to fit that theory. Wrongful conviction — conviction of a person who did not commit the offence, later shown by new evidence."]}
]},
{u:4,l:2,title:"From Arrest to Trial: The Legal Process",sub:"Evidence gathering, disposition of charges, and process vocabulary",sections:[
 {h:"The legal process, step by step",items:["1. Investigation — police gather witness statements, physical evidence, forensic testing, and (where authorized) surveillance or search warrants.","2. Arrest & Charge — police lay a charge if there are reasonable grounds; the accused must be informed of the charge and the right to counsel (s.10).","3. Bail Hearing — a judge or justice of the peace decides on release before trial, balancing public safety against the presumption of innocence.","4. Disclosure & Plea — the Crown must disclose its evidence to the defence; the accused enters a plea. Most criminal cases end in a guilty plea rather than a full trial.","5. Trial & Verdict — the Crown must prove guilt beyond a reasonable doubt; a judge (or judge and jury) delivers a verdict, then sentencing if guilty."]},
 {h:"Evidence: strengths and weak points",items:["Physical and forensic evidence (DNA, fingerprints, ballistics) is treated as highly reliable, but methods vary enormously in validity and some long-used techniques have been discredited.","Eyewitness testimony feels compelling but is one of the least reliable forms: memory is reconstructive, distorted by stress, suggestion, and time.","Confessions carry enormous weight with juries despite well-documented false confessions, especially from young, vulnerable, or intellectually disabled suspects under lengthy or coercive interrogation.","Every wrongful conviction case in this unit involves at least one of these weak points."]},
 {h:"Disposition and vocabulary",items:["Disposition — how a charge is resolved: withdrawn, stayed, guilty plea, or trial. The vast majority of charges are resolved without a full trial, so trial protections like a jury apply to only a small fraction of cases — a coerced confession can lead to a guilty plea that never gets trial scrutiny.","Disclosure — the Crown's obligation to share evidence before trial. Plea bargain — pleading guilty, often to a lesser charge, for a reduced sentence. Alford plea — a guilty plea while maintaining innocence, acknowledging the Crown likely has enough evidence to convict. Recantation — a witness or informant retracting earlier testimony."]}
]},
{u:4,l:3,title:"Wrongful Convictions I: The Central Park Five",sub:"False confessions, tunnel vision, race and justice",sections:[
 {h:"A framework before the case",items:["Wrongful convictions usually involve several compounding factors. The most common (Innocence Project / Innocence Canada): eyewitness misidentification, false or coerced confessions, tunnel vision, flawed or misrepresented forensic science, and jailhouse informant testimony. These recur across three cases in three decades and two countries — not a coincidence."]},
 {h:"The Central Park Jogger case (New York City, April 19, 1989)",items:["A group of teenagers entered Central Park; some committed assaults. Separately, 28-year-old Trisha Meili was attacked, raped, and left for dead while jogging.","Police arrested five Black and Latino teenagers aged 14–16 — Antron McCray, Kevin Richardson, Yusef Salaam, Raymond Santana, and Korey Wise. After hours of aggressive interrogation, mostly without parents or lawyers, all five confessed, implicating each other — despite no physical evidence and significant inconsistencies between statements.","Media branded them a 'wolf pack' engaged in 'wilding', intensifying pressure for swift convictions. All five were convicted and served 6 to 13 years. Textbook tunnel vision: once investigators settled on the five, inconsistencies were interpreted to fit the theory."]},
 {h:"Exoneration — Matias Reyes (December 19, 2002)",items:["Reyes, a convicted serial rapist and murderer serving life, encountered Korey Wise in prison and confessed he alone attacked Meili, providing accurate non-public details. DNA matched Reyes to crime-scene evidence that had never matched the five.","All five convictions were vacated on December 19, 2002. In 2014 the Exonerated Five settled with New York City for $41 million. The 2012 documentary The Central Park Five (Ken Burns, Sarah Burns, David McMahon) told the story from their perspective; the case remains the most cited example of coerced juvenile confessions."]}
]},
{u:4,l:4,title:"Wrongful Convictions II: The West Memphis Three",sub:"Moral panic, junk science, and a coerced confession",sections:[
 {h:"The Robin Hood Hills murders (West Memphis, Arkansas, May 5–6, 1993)",items:["Three eight-year-old boys — Steve Branch, Christopher Byers, and Michael Moore — went missing and were found the next day naked, hogtied with their shoelaces, and beaten in a drainage ditch. A rumour spread that the killings were satanic. This was the height of the 'Satanic Panic' — widespread, largely unfounded fear of hidden cults harming children. That climate, not evidence, pointed investigators toward their suspects."]},
 {h:"From rumour to arrest",items:["Juvenile probation officer Jerry Driver gave police the name of 18-year-old Damien Echols — who wore black, listened to heavy metal, and was interested in Wicca — plus his friends Jason Baldwin and Jessie Misskelley Jr. Vicki Hutcheson told police she could arrange to 'implicate' Echols. No physical evidence connected the three; the case was built first and evidence sought second.","Misskelley, 17, with an IQ around 72, was interrogated about 12 hours with no parent or lawyer for most of it; only minutes were recorded. Chief inspector Gary Gitchell, asked his confidence on a scale of 1 to 10, said '11'. Prosecutor John Fogleman made the confession the centrepiece despite inconsistencies with the known facts."]},
 {h:"Trial, verdicts, and aftermath",items:["Three things closed the case: the Hutchesons' testimony with Misskelley's confession (used against Echols and Baldwin although he did not testify and could not be cross-examined); a knife from a lake behind Baldwin's trailer park that Fogleman and examiner Dr. Frank Peretti claimed matched the wounds (dozens of independent experts later called this outlandish; the crime lab had a conflict of interest); and inmate Michael Carson's claim that Baldwin confessed — later admitted to be a lie told while high.","February–March 1994: Misskelley — life plus two 20-year terms; Baldwin — life without parole; Echols — death.","The Paradise Lost trilogy (Joe Berlinger and Bruce Sinofsky, 1996–2011) and Peter Jackson's West of Memphis (2012) changed the case; Eddie Vedder, Henry Rollins, and Johnny Depp kept it alive; Devil's Knot (2014) dramatized it.","DNA testing from 2007 attributed none of the recovered material to the three. After the Arkansas Supreme Court heard the case (September 30, 2010), an Alford plea (North Carolina v. Alford) released them on August 19, 2011 after 18 years — legally guilty while maintaining innocence, so no one else has been charged and the murders remain officially unsolved."]}
]},
{u:4,l:5,title:"Wrongful Convictions III: David Milgaard",sub:"Canada's own case — and the law it changed",sections:[
 {h:"The murder of Gail Miller (Saskatoon, January 31, 1969)",items:["16-year-old David Milgaard, with friends Ron Wilson and Nichol John, was driving through Saskatoon on a road trip from Regina toward Calgary. That morning nursing aide Gail Miller was raped and murdered nearby. Police investigated more than 160 suspects.","Police pressured Milgaard's teenage companions into statements implicating him — interviews and polygraphs with no adults present. In 1970, at 17, he was convicted and sentenced to life. The same pattern as the American cases: pressured statements from young witnesses rather than physical evidence."]},
 {h:"Larry Fisher and the missed tip",items:["Fisher rented a basement apartment from Albert Cadrain — an acquaintance of Milgaard's and a paid police informant who implicated him. A 1970 investigation showed Fisher had committed several sexual assaults in the same neighbourhood, but he was never seriously pursued. A 1980 tip pointing to Fisher was never followed up.","In 1991, investigator Paul Henderson and Centurion Ministries' Jim McCloskey assembled evidence of the similarities between Fisher's assaults and Miller's murder, and that authorities had not disclosed Fisher's convictions. Clear tunnel vision."]},
 {h:"The long road and exoneration",items:["Joyce Milgaard's two-decade 'epic struggle'. A 1991 application to the Minister of Justice was dismissed in February; a second focused on Fisher. In 1992 the Supreme Court heard the fresh evidence and recommended a new trial — while controversially finding the original trial fair. Saskatchewan chose not to prosecute again; Milgaard was released in April 1992 after nearly 23 years, not yet exonerated.","In 1997 DNA from preserved semen samples matched Fisher, not Milgaard; Saskatchewan apologized. Fisher was arrested in July 1997, convicted in November 1999, and died in prison in 2015. Milgaard received $10 million and on September 9, 2004 was declared 'factually innocent'. He advocated for the wrongfully convicted until his death in 2022."]},
 {h:"How the law changed",items:["Justice Edward MacCallum's Commission of Inquiry identified numerous miscarriages of justice. Before reform, reviews depended on the Minister of Justice's discretion — the same politically accountable process that dismissed the 1991 application.","The David and Joyce Milgaard's Law received Royal Assent, creating an independent Miscarriage of Justice Review Commission — one of the clearest examples of a single case producing permanent structural change. Law is not static; it evolves in response to real failures."]}
]},
{u:4,l:6,title:"International Criminal Law: Nuremberg to the ICC",sub:"The Rome Statute, complementarity, and Canada's leadership",sections:[
 {h:"Nuremberg, revisited (1945–46)",items:["Senior Nazi leaders were prosecuted for crimes including crimes against humanity — a category that did not clearly exist in international law beforehand. Defence lawyers invoked nullum crimen sine lege, 'no crime without a law'.","The tribunal answered with natural law: some acts are so fundamentally wrong that no formal law was needed — which positivists rejected as retroactive punishment. Twelve defendants were sentenced to death; the trials concluded within about a year. The tension resurfaces whenever new atrocity crimes are defined, including in drafting the Rome Statute."]},
 {h:"Two international courts, two jobs",items:["International Court of Justice (ICJ) — settles legal disputes between states (borders, treaty obligations); only with the states' consent; cannot prosecute individuals. Established under the UN Charter in 1945; based in The Hague.","International Criminal Court (ICC) — prosecutes individuals for genocide, crimes against humanity, war crimes, and the crime of aggression. Jurisdiction generally requires the accused's country to be a member, or a UN Security Council referral. A court of last resort (complementarity). Established by the Rome Statute (1998), in force since 2002; also in The Hague."]},
 {h:"The Rome Statute and Canada's leadership",items:["Adopted in Rome July 17, 1998; in force July 1, 2002 once 60 countries ratified; 125 member states today. Canada was the 14th to sign (December 18, 1998). Philippe Kirsch chaired the committee that shaped the Statute's architecture and became the ICC's first president. Canada led the 'Like-Minded Group' of nearly 60 states pushing for a prosecutor independent of the Security Council.","In 2000 Canada became the first country to fully implement the Rome Statute domestically through the Crimes Against Humanity and War Crimes Act. The United States signed but never ratified, and withdrew its signature in 2002."]},
 {h:"Complementarity and criticisms",items:["The ICC steps in only when a country's own courts are unwilling or genuinely unable to prosecute. Canada's domestic Act means it retains the first right to prosecute its own nationals.","Criticisms: no police force of its own — it relies on member states to arrest and transfer suspects; prosecutions focused disproportionately on African states (selective justice); major powers like the U.S., China, and Russia are not members.","Vocabulary: complementarity; crime of aggression (armed force by a state against another state's sovereignty); ratification (formal, legally binding confirmation of a signed treaty); nullum crimen sine lege."]}
]},
{u:4,l:7,title:"Synthesis: Crime, Justice, and Accountability",sub:"Four cases, one pattern, what justice requires",sections:[
 {h:"What went wrong",items:["Central Park Five — coerced juvenile confessions, media-driven tunnel vision, a rush to reassure a frightened public.","West Memphis Three — a moral panic, a coerced confession from a suspect with an intellectual disability, forensic conclusions later called outlandish.","David Milgaard — pressured witness statements, a credible alternative suspect never pursued, a missed police tip left unexamined for a decade."]},
 {h:"What finally corrected it",items:["In every case: new forensic science (DNA testing) unavailable at the original trial. In every case: sustained external pressure — documentaries, advocates, investigators, or family members who refused to let the case close. Only in Milgaard's case: a permanent structural change to how the system reviews future wrongful convictions."]},
 {h:"Rights, responsibilities, and technology",items:["Legal rights (s.7–14) exist to prevent exactly these process failures — the right to counsel, to remain silent, to a fair trial are not abstract. DNA exonerated three of the four people studied; the same technology — DNA databases, surveillance, predictive policing — raises privacy questions the system is still working out.","Whose responsibility is it to revisit old convictions when new technology appears — the defendant, the courts, or the government? Will better technology make wrongful convictions rarer, or will new kinds of errors take their place?"]},
 {h:"From the individual to the international",items:["The unit moved from one accused person in one trial to an international court built to hold governments and leaders accountable. Both ends share the same demand: guilt established through fair process, not public pressure, political convenience, or a rush to closure.","Complementarity assumes domestic justice systems are the first and most important line of defence — so the first five lessons are the foundation the international system depends on. 'The System Is Only as Just as Its Weakest Case.'"]}
]}
];
